    {
      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the degree measure of an angle whose measure is double the measure of its complement, let's denote the measure of the angle by \\( x \\).\n\nFirst, recall that the complement of an angle is what, when added to the angle, equals 90 degrees. Therefore, the complement of the angle \\( x \\) is \\( 90^\\circ - x \\).\n\nAccording to the problem, the measure of the angle is double the measure of its complement. This can be written as:\n\\[ x = 2(90^\\circ - x) \\]\n\nNow, we will solve this equation step by step.\n\n1. Distribute the 2 on the right side of the equation:\n\\[ x = 180^\\circ - 2x \\]\n\n2. Add \\( 2x \\) to both sides to isolate the \\( x \\) terms on one side:\n\\[ x + 2x = 180^\\circ \\]\n\\[ 3x = 180^\\circ \\]\n\n3. Divide both sides by 3 to solve for \\( x \\):\n\\[ x = \\frac{180^\\circ}{3} \\]\n\\[ x = 60^\\circ \\]\n\nTherefore, the degree measure of the angle is \\(\\boxed{60}\\).",
